Will You “Keep Walking by Spirit ”?
What Is God’s Kingdom?
When people listened to him speak, they surely heard many references to that Kingdom. Were they puzzled or mystified?
So was God’s Kingdom a familiar concept to those people? The fact is, the ancient Scriptures that the Jews revered as holy described that Kingdom, revealing in vivid and concrete terms what it is and what it will accomplish.
The prophet Daniel was given a vision in which he saw a succession of world powers, stretching down through history into our own time.
God’s Kingdom will end wars, sickness, famine, even death itself. Thrilling Bible prophecies reveal what God’s Kingdom will do here on the earth.
Who knows better than our Creator what kind of Ruler we need? The Ruler of God’s Kingdom has demonstrated his worthiness before all mankind.
Well, God spoke from heaven, identifying him as His own Son; angels pointed Jesus out as the foretold Messiah; and Jesus performed miracles — often in front of hundreds or even thousands of eyewitnesses — that clearly drew on the power of God. * Jesus showed over and over again what kind of Ruler he would be.
The Bible gives ample evidence that Jesus has been granted his authority as King in heaven.
He is ruling there now, in our own time, and very soon he will extend his rule to all the earth and fulfill the magnificent prophecies we have already mentioned. But how can we be sure that God’s Kingdom is ruling now?
And when will it begin to rule over the earth?
Today, some 2,000 years later, people are still eager to know: When will God’s Kingdom come?
Since Jesus made the Kingdom the theme of his preaching, you might expect that he discussed this question.
And indeed he did! He spoke extensively about a marked period of time that he called his “presence. ”
Let us consider four truths the Bible reveals about the presence of Christ. Christ’s presence would begin a long time after his death.
Jesus gave an illustration in which he likened himself to a man who “traveled to a distant land to secure kingly power, ” or to“ secure a kingdom. ”
Well, Jesus died and was resurrected; then he traveled to the “distant land, ” that is, heaven. As Jesus foretold in a similar illustration, his return in kingly power would come only “after a long time. ” — Matthew 25: 19.
Some years after Jesus ascended to heaven, the apostle Paul wrote: “This man offered one sacrifice for sins perpetually and sat down at the right hand of God, from then on awaiting until his enemies should be placed as a stool for his feet. ” So a lengthy period of waiting followed Jesus ’ arrival in heaven.
Why have a sign to point out what you can easily identify with your eyes?
Jesus described the sign of his presence, not to point out something that humans could see with their eyes, but to help them discern something that would occur in heaven. Thus, Jesus said: “The kingdom of God is not coming with striking observableness. ”
Jesus ’ presence would be marked by a time of profound troubles here on earth. Jesus said that his presence as King in heaven would be marked on earth by wars, famines, earthquakes, pestilences, and lawlessness.
Such visible evidence of Satan’s rage and of Christ’s presence has been abundant in our time. Especially since 1914, a year that historians acknowledge was a turning point, has this evidence appeared on an unprecedented and global scale.
All of that may sound like bad news, but it is not.
Jesus said that his followers on earth would be doing something similar during his presence. He prophesied: “This good news of the kingdom will be preached in all the inhabited earth for a witness to all the nations; and then the end will come. ” — Matthew 24: 14.
Will God’s Kingdom Mean Good News for You?
Some translations render it “coming, ”“ advent, ” or “return, ” all of which imply a momentary event in time. Notice, though, that Jesus did not liken his presence to the Flood of Noah’s day, an event in time, but to “the days of Noah, ” a climactic period of time.
The bad news we hear every day proves that good things are coming soon
