Noting that the brother has received specific counsel in the past but has failed to improve, the visiting circuit overseer agrees and joins in the recommendation that he be deleted as an elder.
Or will he harbor resentment and jealousy over no longer having the privilege that he once had?
Why would a brother act as if he qualified as an older man when in reality he did not?
Why do you feel that the inspired counsel of James 3: 15, 16 is so important? Granted, there are other ways that a similar attitude could appear.
How unfitting for a Christian to display those characteristics!
Qualities That the Wise Seek to Display
Being chaste involves being pure and undefiled in our actions and motives. We need to reject evil things immediately.
This involves our avoiding aggressiveness, a belligerent attitude, or actions that would disrupt peace.
James expands on this point when he says: “The fruit of righteousness has its seed sown under peaceful conditions for those who are making peace. ”
Notice the expression “making peace. ”
In the congregation, are we known as peacemakers or as peacebreakers?
Do we frequently have differences or disagreements with others, being easily offended or offending others?
Do we insist that others accept us as we are, or do we humbly work to eliminate personality traits that others rightly find offensive? Are we known to go out of our way to promote peace, being quick to forgive and putting errors behind us?
How does one come to be known as a reasonable person?
James included being reasonable in the description of what reflects the wisdom from above. Are we known for being inclined to give in to others when no Scriptural principle is involved, not being quick to insist that our own personal standards be followed?
Do we have a reputation for being gentle and easy to talk to?
These are indications that we have learned to be reasonable.
What will result as we manifest the godly qualities just discussed?
What delightful conditions can exist in a congregation as brothers and sisters work at displaying ever more the godly qualities about which James wrote!
Is it your automatic reaction to reject evil?
Do You View Others as Jehovah Does?
WHEN we first came out of the wicked world and began associating with Jehovah’s people, we were likely delighted to experience the warm love and care that exist among them.
What a contrast to the coarse, hateful, and contentious people under Satan’s control!
He loves and appreciates each member.
Do we really believe that Jehovah is leading his congregated people and that if adjustments need to be made, he will take action in his due time? — Read Romans 14: 1 - 4.
He also knows what spiritual progress they have already made.
Therefore, he has no reason to focus on each fleshly weakness.
How thankful we can personally be for that! — Ps.
Still, Jehovah chose to focus on the good because he knew that David’s heart was upright. — 1 Chron.
See Your Brothers as Jehovah Does
We do not know all of another person’s motives. We should try to imitate Jehovah by not fixing our attention on human imperfections, which will eventually disappear.
To illustrate, think of a house that is run - down — the gutters are falling off, windows are broken, and ceiling panels are water damaged. Most people might look at that house and conclude that it should be torn down; it is an eyesore.
Rather than focusing on our brothers ’ surface defects, can we discern their good qualities and their potential for further spiritual growth? If we do so, we will come to love our brothers for their spiritual beauty, as Jehovah does. — Read Hebrews 6: 10.
The apostle Paul gave some advice that can help us in our relationships with all in the congregation.
What changes have affected some congregations?
Cultivating genuine love and affection can be a challenge in a multinational setting.
Our fellow worshippers ’ culture may vary considerably from our own, as may their educational, economic, and ethnic background. Do you find it a challenge to understand the thinking or reactions of some?
Do we ever catch ourselves saying something like “I am not prejudiced, but... ” and then recounting some negative characteristics that we consider to be common among members of a certain ethnic group? Such feelings may reveal the need for us to rid ourselves of prejudice that we still harbor deep down.
Such self - examination may help us make improvements in accepting and appreciating our international brotherhood.
Saul, who later became the apostle Paul, also had to make changes and rid himself of prejudice. He admitted that he had so hated the Christians that “to the point of excess kept on persecuting the congregation of God and devastating it. ”
Still, when the Lord Jesus corrected Paul, he made big changes and even began to accept direction from those whom he formerly persecuted. — Gal.
