The House - to - House Ministry — Why Important Now?
Indeed, the house - to - house ministry has become our trademark.
We use a variety of methods to carry out the commission Jesus gave to preach and make disciples.
What is the basis for this method of preaching? How did it come to be used so extensively by God’s people in modern times?
The method of preaching from house to house has its basis in the Scriptures. When Jesus sent forth the apostles to preach, he instructed them: “Into whatever city or village you enter, search out who in it is deserving. ”
Jesus told them to go to people’s homes, saying: “When you are entering into the house, greet the household; and if the house is deserving, let the peace you wish it come upon it. ”
Note Jesus ’ further words: “Wherever anyone does not take you in or listen to your words, on going out of that house or that city shake the dust off your feet. ” These instructions make clear that as the apostles “went through the territory from village to village, declaring the good news, ” they were to take the initiative to visit people in their homes. — Luke 9: 6.
How is the preaching work depicted in the prophecy of Joel?
The prophet Joel likened the preaching activity of anointed Christians to a devastating plague of insects, including locusts.
Foremost among the methods used by anointed Christians and their “other sheep ” companions in fulfilling this prophetic picture is the house - to - house ministry.
They raised various objections, but the basic problem was that some felt it beneath their dignity to preach from house to house.
In the decades that followed, the scope of the preaching work grew.
The importance of the house - to - house ministry can be seen from a vision given to the prophet Ezekiel. In that vision, Ezekiel sees six men with weapons in their hands as well as a seventh man clothed in linen with a secretary’s inkhorn at his side.
How does Ezekiel’s vision about marking foreheads highlight the importance of our ongoing search for sheeplike ones?
Concerning that coming judgment, the apostle Paul wrote that the Lord Jesus, accompanied by “his powerful angels, ” will bring“ vengeance upon those who do not know God and those who do not obey the good news about our Lord Jesus. ”
What developments are yet in store for the preaching work?
Recall that just before God destroyed Jericho, the Israelites were instructed to march around the city once a day for six days.
It is possible that there will be a similar expansion of our preaching activity. No doubt, by the time of the destruction of the present system of things, we will have seen the greatest witness to God’s name and Kingdom in the history of this world.
As we await the momentous events that lie ahead, may we continue zealously declaring the good news of the Kingdom.
