A Conspiracy Against Jehovah’s People
According to the superscription, Psalm 83 is “a melody of Asaph. ”
The composer of the psalm was likely a descendant of the Levite Asaph, a prominent musician during the reign of King David. In the psalm, the psalmist begs Jehovah to take action so as to uphold His sovereignty and make His name known.
Because during the reigns of David and Solomon, Tyre’s king was friendly toward Israel. By the time Psalm 83 was composed, the inhabitants of Tyre had turned against Israel and had sided with its enemies.
The psalmist names ten nations that were conspiring to destroy God’s people. Those enemies were situated all around Israel and are listed as follows: “The tents of Edom and the Ishmaelites, Moab and the Hagrites, Gebal and Ammon and Amalek, Philistia together with the inhabitants of Tyre.
Some suggest that the psalm refers to the attack on Israel by the coalition of Ammon, Moab, and the inhabitants of Mount Seir in the days of Jehoshaphat.
Others believe that it speaks of the general hostility Israel experienced from its neighbors throughout its history.
A Matter of Primary Concern
your very enemies are in an uproar; and the very ones intensely hating you have raised their head.
What was the psalmist’s foremost concern?
