JESUS once asked his disciples: “Which father is there among you who, if his son asks for a fish, will perhaps hand him a serpent instead of a fish?
Or if he also asks for an egg, will hand him a scorpion? ”
The children of Galilee enjoyed eating eggs and fish; they knew what they wanted.
Jesus said that we should keep on asking persistently for the holy spirit, like a hungry child asking for food. Understanding the nature of the holy spirit will enable us to grasp the key role it can play in our lives.
Jesus promised his disciples: “You will receive power when the holy spirit arrives upon you. ”
The holy spirit is the means by which Jehovah exerts his power. Put simply, the holy spirit is God’s applied power, or his active force.
And what a force that is! We cannot comprehend the power needed to create the entire universe.
The ministry of Jesus gives us a fascinating glimpse of how God’s holy spirit can empower his servants. “ Jehovah’s spirit is upon me, ” Jesus told the people of Nazareth.
The apostle Peter described Jesus as “a man publicly shown by God... through powerful works and portents and signs that God did through him. ” — Acts 2: 22.
Why the Holy Spirit Is Not a Person
The Bible compares the holy spirit to water. When promising future blessings for his people, God said: “I shall pour out water upon the thirsty one, and trickling streams upon the dry place.
Consider this: Could a person be ‘ poured out ’ on many different individuals?
Would you say that one person could ‘ fill ’ a whole group of people? That defies logic.
The Bible does refer to people becoming filled with wisdom, understanding, or even accurate knowledge, but it never describes anyone as being filled with another person. — Exodus 28: 3; 1 Kings 7: 14; Luke 2: 40; Colossians 1: 9.
The Greek word translated “spirit ” is pneuʹma, which also conveys the idea of an invisible power.
Clearly, then, the holy spirit is not a person.
For more information, see “The Truth About the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit, ” pages 201 - 204, in the book What Does the Bible Really Teach?
