The characters they copied were fictional, but the liquor Allen and his friends drank was real. Tony was 40 when he gradually increased his drinks from one or two each evening to five or six.
Allen is alive today to tell his story; Tony died some years ago in a motor vehicle accident after drinking too much liquor.
If you choose to drink alcohol, how much is too much?
How can you know when not to have “just one more drink ”?
What Is God’s View of Alcohol?
Through the pages of the Bible, our heavenly Father ‘ teaches us to benefit ourselves, ’ and he strongly cautions us against the abuse of strong drink.
Let us now consider some of the dangers of drinking too much alcohol. Dangers of Overdrinking
Overuse of alcohol can cause any of the following problems: Heavy drinking impairs the drinker’s judgment, so that he ‘ will not be able to think clearly. ’
Allen, the heavy drinker mentioned in the preceding article, explains: “Alcoholism is not just a disease of the body; it’s a disease of one’s thinking and attitudes.
Overdrinking can make you “feel as if you were out on the ocean, seasick, swinging high up in the rigging of a tossing ship. ” A man who has overindulged can wake up “bruised all over, without even remembering how it all happened. ” — Proverbs 23: 35, CEV.
The most serious consequences of overindulgence, though, are not described in medical textbooks.
The Bible plainly says: “Woe to those who are getting up early in the morning that they may seek just intoxicating liquor, who are lingering till late in the evening darkness so that wine itself inflames them! ”
Isaiah explains the spiritual consequences of immoderate drinking: “The activity of Jehovah they do not look at, and the work of his hands they have not seen. ” — Isaiah 5: 11, 12.
God’s Word advises us not “to be among heavy drinkers of wine. ”
Slowly — often imperceptibly — people start consuming a little more alcohol with increasing frequency. Eventually, the drinker may “lie awake asking, ‘ When will morning come, so I can drink some more? ’ ”
What, though, can immoderate drinkers do to prevent becoming ‘ weighed down with heavy drinking ’?
Overuse of alcohol can lead to many problems
Keeping Alcohol in Its Place
Whether Tony realized it or not, the very organ that monitored his physical, mental, and emotional condition — his brain — was malfunctioning when it was overloaded with alcohol.
Take Action!
Many who have stopped abusing alcohol have taken action in line with Jesus ’ words: “If, now, that right eye of yours is making you stumble, tear it out and throw it away from you. For it is more beneficial to you for one of your members to be lost to you than for your whole body to be pitched into Gehenna. ” — Matthew 5: 29.
As painful as that may be, it is much less painful than a ruined life. Even if you are not an alcoholic, do you tend to drink too much?
If so, what practical steps can you take to help you keep alcohol in its place?
Where to Get Help
The Bible offers this advice to all who want to please Jehovah God: “In everything by prayer and supplication along with thanksgiving let your petitions be made known to God; and the peace of God that excels all thought will guard your hearts and your mental powers by means of Christ Jesus. ”
How, though, can you work in harmony with such prayers, and where can you find the answers to your supplications?
The Bible reports that former drunkards in the Christian congregation had been washed clean “with the spirit of our God. ”
For one thing, they were helped to give up drunken bouts and revelries by cultivating self - control, a quality that is developed with the help of God’s holy spirit.
Hence, if you choose to drink, it is not wise to set a vague limit that hovers between sobriety and drunkenness. Do not let denial undermine your honest appraisal of your drinking.
Breaking Free From Addiction
Allen pauses and holds back the tears as memories overwhelm him. “ Um,... to know that Jehovah understands and cares and provides the needed help — that has been incredible. ”
Therefore, if you are in the grip of alcohol misuse or addiction, do not be quick to give up on yourself or to conclude that there is no hope. Allen and countless others have been where you are now and have either cut down on alcohol or have cut it out altogether.
They have no regrets; neither will you. Whether you personally choose to drink alcohol in moderation or not at all, heed God’s loving appeal: “O if only you would actually pay attention to my commandments!
See the box “Is Alcohol Taking Control? ”
Each individual should carefully evaluate treatment options and then make a personal decision that does not conflict with Bible principles.
Is Alcohol Taking Control?
You might ask yourself:
If you answer yes to one or more of these questions, you may need to take steps to control your drinking.
Making Wise Decisions About Alcohol
Before drinking alcohol, consider:
Recommendation: One who cannot limit his drinks should abstain.
Recommendation: Determine your limit before alcohol distorts your judgment.
Recommendations: Not before driving or engaging in activities that require alertness; not before engaging in religious activities; not during pregnancy; not with certain medications.
Recommendations: In a wholesome setting; not in secret to hide the drinking; not in front of people who are offended by alcohol.
Recommendations: With wholesome friends or family; not with problem drinkers.
God’s Word Helps a Former Drunkard
Can the Dead Help the Living?
Across the globe, the belief that the dead can help the living persists. What do you think?
It is only natural to long for loved ones whom we have lost in death.
Are the Dead Alive Somewhere?
Note what Ecclesiastes 9: 5 states: “The living are conscious that they will die; but as for the dead, they are conscious of nothing at all. ”
Jesus helped many while he was alive, but he knew that he would have to die. Did he expect that he would still be able to help people during his time in the grave?
He compared his own impending death to a nighttime when work would be impossible. Jesus well knew that when humans cease to live, they are “impotent in death. ” — Isaiah 26: 14.
We do not expect a sleeping person to help us, do we? A sleeper is unconscious, unable to act in anyone’s behalf.
Does the Soul Survive Death?
However, some may ask, ‘ What about the many stories of people contacting the dead, hearing their voices, or even seeing them? ’
They fill bereaved relatives or close friends with hope, moving many to seek out a spirit medium who claims to be able to contact the dead.
Are such stories true?
If so, would this not contradict the above Bible - based statements?
A King Asks for Help From the Dead
The Bible states that at death a person “goes back to his ground ” and that“ his thoughts do perish. ” Both Saul and Samuel knew that God condemned contact with spirit mediums.
Reason on the matter. If faithful Samuel were still alive as a spirit, would he violate God’s law and cooperate with a spirit medium in order to meet Saul?
Jehovah had refused to talk with Saul. Could a medium, in effect, force God Almighty to communicate with Saul by means of dead Samuel?
Surely not!
Real Help for the Living and the Dead
We understand from the foregoing that the dead are powerless to help the living. What is more, efforts to seek the help of the dead are worse than ineffective; they are extremely dangerous, as they violate God’s law and put us at risk of demonic influence.
He had learned the real condition of the dead and also the evil source of those who pretend to be dead ancestors. Despite great pressure from his mother to get help from a medium, Tamba refused, telling her, “If I fail, I will try harder next year. ”
What, though, about the loved ones we have lost?
Is there no hope for them?
The prophecy further states that “those impotent in death ” will live again.
Imagine that!
The helpless billions who lie sleeping in the grave will be brought back to life!
In fact, the Bible reveals that Jehovah has “a yearning ” to give life to the dead.
Do such promises sound too good to be true? Jesus Christ was so sure of this hope that he described the dead as already alive in Jehovah’s eyes. — Luke 20: 37, 38.
Would you like to make this hope your own?
Name has been changed.
